A||N||11||HummelHund||Brake Line Repair or Replacement? 84||||5||
Z||000000||HummelHund||09-10-2001||01:24 PM||||We recently picked up a nice 84 SE. Two problems - one, the fuel pump is kaput, but I'll get that done. The bigger - the brake booster and M/C were missing! And the brake lines going to the valve were broken off. I went to a yard to see how had it would be to grab a set - too tough. So, how to replace the missing pieces? I need from just after the rubber separator to the proportioning valve. Any hints or sources for something like this??? TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||artherd||09-10-2001||08:14 PM||||If they have been sitting open, REPLACE THE WHOLE SYSTEM.  Nothing else will do, maybe you can rebuild the calipers.  Everything else needs replacenemt, unless you want to see what a concreate embankment looks like from the inside out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>It is fairly easy to bend up new lines, and you make the ends with a "double flare" tool. not hard at all.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by HummelHund:<BR><B>We recently picked up a nice 84 SE. Two problems - one, the fuel pump is kaput, but I'll get that done. The bigger - the brake booster and M/C were missing! And the brake lines going to the valve were broken off. I went to a yard to see how had it would be to grab a set - too tough. So, how to replace the missing pieces? I need from just after the rubber separator to the proportioning valve. Any hints or sources for something like this??? TIA</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000002||HummelHund||09-10-2001||08:14 PM||||====bump=====||||reg||1
Z||000003||Leadfoot||09-10-2001||11:36 PM||||Artherd is right, you should replace the line.  I had the rear brake line rust out on me.  As a quick fix...I DON'T RECOMMEND IT THOUGH.....you can go to NAPA and they sell some super duty steel unions (some really beefy bastards).  They told me it was legal as long as the components are not brass, or copper, but steel (wouldn't be the first time they were wrong).  I intend to replace the lines this winter.  Til then I don't slam on the brakes and give myself plenty of braking distance. This is one of those "do as I say and not as I do" replies.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||cadaver||09-11-2001||11:09 PM||||I just did a complete brake line replacement on my Mustang. Thats right a Mustang. I did all the hard lines for around $20 with parts available from better parts stores.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DreXteR||09-12-2001||10:01 AM||||Damn!! I posted same problem 2 times and dont get any answer like this ones. Makeing the  hard lines for $20!! I just bid a set of lines on ebay for $168  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Busted!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000006||HummelHund||09-13-2001||12:21 AM||||As in which better stores? Including the tools to bend and flare?<P>TIA,<P>Hund<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B>I just did a complete brake line replacement on my Mustang. Thats right a Mustang. I did all the hard lines for around $20 with parts available from better parts stores.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierospeeder||09-13-2001||04:00 AM||||i think its classic tube, that had a set for the fiero.  It includes all the steel lines prebent according to the factory.  I think it costs 100-200 bucks, i cant recall.<P>You can go to the carparts store, and buy already flared brake line for 1-5 bucks, depending on length and diameter, which includes the brake fittings.  You can buy a line bender at the parts store, or get one from a hardware store, which usually carry a better kind.||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||09-13-2001||06:19 AM||||If you don't have the (fairly specialised) tools, it will cost you a few $hundred, mostly for said tools  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  If you don't know what you're doing, I suggest either working with a friend who does, so you can learn!, or paying a professional you trust.<P>Think of it as an oppertunity to take what could be a very questionable decades old system that YOUR LIFE and MY LIFE depend on, and replace it entirely with new components that we can trust  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||cadaver||09-13-2001||10:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>If you don't have the (fairly specialised) tools, it will cost you a few $hundred, mostly for said tools   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A few hundred???? Ah no. But hey do you want to buy some tools I have some for sale?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||HummelHund||09-13-2001||11:46 PM||||From what I've gathered so far, all I'll need is the tool to bend the lines. Carquest has the lines, 60" for $4.00 with fittings already installed - no flaring required, as well as shorter lengths that join together as required. J C Whitney has two types of "benders", most expensive about ten bucks. So I figure $35 parts, plus bender. Take my time, save $150. On a $100 car, we tend to look for the cheapest effective way to solve the problem.<P>Hund<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B> A few hundred???? Ah no. But hey do you want to buy some tools I have some for sale?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierospeeder||09-14-2001||01:46 AM||||go to <A HREF="http://www.harborfreight.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.harborfreight.com</A> <P>and they have a flaring tool for about 10 bucks and a bending tool for around 7-8 bucks.<P>Your gonna spend like 40 bucks buying a flaring tool from a car store.  You can also buy a good one of ebay.||||reg||1
